        {
          "id": "tank-8",
          "title": "X-CMS",
          "added": "2026-08-27",
          "description": "I built a simple household knowledge hub that held important information for major ongoing projects in our lives, which really worked great for some time. To update it I would have Claude generate an HTML page for each new item, and now with proven utility and some minor increase in scale, I decided I wanted to make my own mini content management system (because, of course, as one does). I've become a markdown queen in the last few years, so I figure what would work best for my workflow was a local UI that could WYSIWYG my markdown files, then build and ship them. I put together a simple, bold, distinctive template that I liked and worked well on all viewport widths, and now I can throw together some research/reference documents quickly using md files and get them into our household webhome. It's got a simple auth and middleware so it's obviously not the Pentagon, but it is a functional gate that stops humans and robots snooping. I love it and use it all the time.",
          "tech": ["Node.js", "Markdown", "TypeScript", "Vercel"],
